<section>
    <h3>Recording an error response</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php" data-trim data-line-numbers="1-8|1-2|4-6">
// Not recorded: the exception consumes the response
$response->getContent();   // ClientException on a 404

// Recorded with its status and its body
$response->getContent(false);
$response->toArray(false);
    </code></pre>
    <p class="fragment callout warn">
        Only <b>fully received</b> responses are written. A response destroyed without being read, cancelled,
        or consumed through the exception is not recorded: on replay, the test fails clearly instead of receiving an empty body.
    </p>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>First pitfall: error responses.</p>
        <p>[click] If you call getContent() on a 404 response, HttpClient throws a ClientException. And that exception consumes the response: it isn't recorded.</p>
        <p>[click] If you want to record an error response, pass false: getContent(false) or toArray(false). The response is then recorded with its status and its body.</p>
        <p>[click] The general rule: only fully received responses are written. A response destroyed without being read, cancelled, or consumed by the exception isn't recorded. That's on purpose: on replay, the test fails clearly, instead of silently receiving an empty body.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
